b/ref 17592186402349} {:answer/id #uuid "636fc155-4188-5530-8e60-adb2d62235ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402350} {:answer/id #uuid "41d9b870-5da0-57d1-8c46-b51e8740ed9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402351} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca2db5b-65de-5d3d-a904-b52ac412e5a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41d9b870-5da0-57d1-8c46-b51e8740ed9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402351} :answer-field/id #uuid "819aa321-67db-51f3-9563-c852982e8e87" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402348} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71109050-5463-5ad1-a2e0-992ca3f99166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402354} {:answer/id #uuid "bc720c6e-5092-5a46-980f-2cf9b11293e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71109050-5463-5ad1-a2e0-992ca3f99166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402354} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d6cd00a-0022-5089-9075-29d1a852af1f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09760520-d150-5b51-9f43-20d7cea8711d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343446" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ -2, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1-\\frac{12}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1-\\frac{12}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ -2 & =1-\\frac{12}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1} \\\\ -3 & =-\\frac{12}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4a6755d-80bd-5611-a557-59ce2c3c6557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402525} {:answer/id #uuid "e5dd66e7-ae53-5b28-9e79-614c071a936b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402526} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd50123-6242-511d-b159-5c464286ace9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402527} {:answer/id #uuid "dae85668-9796-5178-b243-6fa5585b42bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$  and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402528} {:answer/id #uuid "7543e93c-1834-59e6-825e-f87499d53fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dae85668-9796-5178-b243-6fa5585b42bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$  and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402528} :answer-field/id #uuid "c165be63-7aaf-561e-9d48-4500807ec533" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "73d12e91-b996-5b04-bc26-e6f1ba8817f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-37088" :question/problem "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ using its characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-6-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-(8)(-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -24 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-(-4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f93a90-917d-59e1-ad77-0b9aad541d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438266} {:answer/id #uuid "12af740c-a997-5387-8362-5e2cc16732ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53f93a90-917d-59e1-ad77-0b9aad541d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438266} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b6fcdaf-3dd3-5706-a56b-12ba3e57c541" :answer-field/key "$N$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439162} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80660670-28fa-562c-9cdd-803d3f3b6e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438268} {:answer/id #uuid "5834e804-b776-587a-9eff-2c1a6107cc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5834e804-b776-587a-9eff-2c1a6107cc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438269} :answer-field/id #uuid "676ac767-8dfd-50fa-9ecf-b51ab8fab0b5" :answer-field/key "$N$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439163} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56d97f56-3c64-5231-b1a7-ace7dd7012d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438270} {:answer/id #uuid "1c459c6d-1c91-560e-9b29-0f2524d0be20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56d97f56-3c64-5231-b1a7-ace7dd7012d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438270} :answer-field/id #uuid "091267d0-c528-519a-8b1c-763f1c12a047" :answer-field/key "$det(N)=-1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5eb8a6b-37c6-54e6-b340-18101dbf3f8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38187" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{7} & 3 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 4 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 5 & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} & 3 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 4 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 5 & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}|\\begin{aligned}4 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 5 & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}|+(-\\frac{1}{7})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} & 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(-\\frac{4}{7}+\\frac{5}{7})-3(-\\frac{1}{49}+\\frac{1}{49})-\\frac{1}{7}(\\frac{5}{7}-\\frac{4}{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}-3\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{49}-\\frac{1}{49} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$N$ is a singular matrix” is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0a0d5ce-ea74-563c-8226-ef12abfcf57c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402725} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca6c567-4159-5b02-87ac-496a7d90cc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402726} {:answer/id #uuid "af2e493c-45e0-51f4-9bd1-9e4936ea0db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Inconclusive because $|r|=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402727} {:answer/id #uuid "419f3375-7f3d-502c-ad8d-fae3914d69e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402728} {:answer/id #uuid "ef235626-3ba2-5405-8b4e-45af49053e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|<1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ca6c567-4159-5b02-87ac-496a7d90cc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402726} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc63abdb-8696-5444-ac6a-673734bc4695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ecdef7ff-1e8e-5448-88b3-81c3a4f39ef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-40579" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }0.12{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3n}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.12{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3(2)}}{0.12{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3(1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{6}}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5408298f-7273-5f8e-b08d-eb9052a72e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=2\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403074} {:answer/id #uuid "55c297c3-ce19-541b-8f15-0e1cff269acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403075} {:answer/id #uuid "18ad6631-816d-504b-86a5-d5fe74a70d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403076} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc7cd2a-4140-5747-8785-3c2be9dddaf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,x=3\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403077} {:answer/id #uuid "ef782508-1876-5861-8688-45bcea667f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=2,\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18ad6631-816d-504b-86a5-d5fe74a70d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403076} :answer-field/id #uuid "838eec31-f001-5f3e-95ff-aee58026f6f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1ac9dcc-ee5b-5438-8680-0a3c7af8b759" :question/math-academy-id "q-60145" :question/problem "The equation $-20{x}^{2}+60x-41=0$ can be written in the form $20(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-20{x}^{2}+60x-41) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 20{x}^{2}-60x+41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{x}^{2}-60x+41 & =0 \\\\ 20{x}^{2}-60x & =-41 \\\\ 20({x}^{2}-3x) & =-41 \\\\ 20[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}] & =-41 \\\\ 20[{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}] & =-41\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-20\\cdot \\frac{9}{4} & =-41 \\\\ 20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-45 & =-41 \\\\ 20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =45-41 \\\\ 20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=4.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{20} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{5}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{3}{2}| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{5}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae3561cc-7a36-53c3-8463-a08b60f2e422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403081} {:answer/id #uuid "9c0c8fbf-a096-54ef-a916-0cf279874a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403082} {:answer/id #uuid "84b429b9-e24f-5b71-bb06-7a7076b1bd11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403083} {:answer/id #uuid "c808c746-284d-54ac-ab48-93af25059dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403084} {:answer/id #uuid "06498b0f-3db5-5fe1-b4f1-27d65e29f54e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06498b0f-3db5-5fe1-b4f1-27d65e29f54e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403085} :answer-field/id #uuid "5281993a-3636-5f8e-a209-1d71b7850d4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a7884f70-6bc5-5884-9b9e-474178b4363b" :question/math-academy-id "q-60356" :question/problem "The equation $-3{x}^{2}-2x+6=0$ can be written in the form $3(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-3{x}^{2}-2x+6) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2x-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+2x-6 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2x & =6 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}x] & =6 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}x+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}] & =6 \\\\ 3[{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}] & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{9} & =6 \\\\ 3{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3} & =6 \\\\ 3{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & =6+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & =\\frac{19}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{1}{3}$, $q=\\frac{19}{3}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\nq-p=\\frac{19}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{18}{3}=6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bfa91e7-ae5d-50b6-a073-b1f80896d1fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403442} {:answer/id #uuid "1e88bd92-da6c-54d3-aa02-4b44560aa8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(2,1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403443} {:answer/id #uuid "44d63414-8671-5d77-9ee7-bc14c9183ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403444} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc17341-08ec-5304-938b-b53f6c9990cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1,-1)$ and $(1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403445} {:answer/id #uuid "32211501-cf71-57e9-a95f-ae04c3650a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44d63414-8671-5d77-9ee7-bc14c9183ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(1,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403444} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a93a4ae-2761-5844-9076-70de06e6a2f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b828b568-be9f-5fe6-8a19-4f466a780e15" :question/math-academy-id "q-71131" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}=1$ and the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1+t \\\\ y &=t, \\\\ z &=-t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2(1+t)^{2}+6(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2(1+2t+{t}^{2})+6{t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2+4t+2{t}^{2}+6{t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 9{t}^{2}+4t & =0 \\\\ t(9t+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{4}{9}$ and $t=0.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{4}{9}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1+(-\\frac{4}{9})=\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ y &=-\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ z &=-(-\\frac{4}{9})=\\frac{4}{9},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9}).$\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1+0=1 \\\\ y &=0 \\\\ z &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(1,0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(1,0,0).$"}]
